# LibreWolf no-nimbus.patch  (Android)
# Owner: LW-M4-03 ("Disable Nimbus experiments and the experimenter endpoint")
#
# Upstream files touched
# ----------------------
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/nimbus.fml.yaml
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/experiments/NimbusSetup.kt
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/HomeActivity.kt
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/test/java/org/mozilla/fenix/nimbus/NimbusSystemTest.kt
#       (added by LW-M4-13 -- see "THE TEST CASUALTY" below)
#
# The first three are the same in firefox-153.0.4 and firefox-153.0esr (the base
# ./version.android pins), so the same hunks apply to either tree. Verified by
# extracting them from firefox-153.0esr.source.tar.xz and diffing against the
# 153.0.4 tree: NimbusSetup.kt and HomeActivity.kt are byte-identical,
# nimbus.fml.yaml differs only in the unrelated `shake-to-summarize` defaults and
# not by a single line of offset, so the fission block sits at the same line
# numbers in both. The whole android list was then applied to a real extracted
# 153.0esr tree (`./scripts/check-patchfail.sh --targets=android`), and these
# three files applied with zero fuzz.
#
#
# What Nimbus is, and what LibreWolf wants instead
# ------------------------------------------------
# Nimbus is Mozilla's remote-configuration and experiment-enrolment channel. In
# Fenix it decides feature flags at runtime, including several that are security
# decisions rather than product experiments. A browser that periodically asks a
# Mozilla server "which experiments am I in" is two bad things at once: an
# upstream-controlled configuration channel we do not audit, and a recurring
# beacon whose timing, TLS fingerprint and client attributes identify the
# installation even when the response is empty. LibreWolf keeps the feature
# manifest -- the compiled-in FML defaults -- and removes the channel.
#
# There is no "experimenter" hostname to block, and this surprises people. In
# 153 the Nimbus client does not talk to experimenter.services.mozilla.com; it
# reads the `nimbus-mobile-experiments` collection from the ordinary
# remote-settings service, i.e.
#   https://firefox.settings.services.mozilla.com/v1/buckets/main/collections/nimbus-mobile-experiments/records
# (third_party/application-services/components/remote_settings/src/config.rs,
# RemoteSettingsServer::Prod). Grepping a capture for "experimenter" therefore
# finds nothing even in a stock build; grep for the collection name.
#
#
# How the channel is actually severed (three independent barriers)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. `serverSettings = null`, unconditionally.
#    NimbusServerSettings is what create_client()
#    (third_party/application-services/components/nimbus/src/stateful/client/mod.rs)
#    switches on: the Some(..) arm calls RemoteSettingsService::make_client()
#    for the experiment collection, the None arm builds a NullClient whose
#    fetch_experiments() returns an empty vec without touching the network.
#    NullClient's own doc comment says it exists "for non-Mozilla forks of apps
#    that are not using their own server infrastructure" -- exactly this case.
#
#    This also removes the collection from the periodic sync. The Rust
#    RemoteSettingsService::sync() iterates active_clients(), which are only the
#    clients that make_client() created; with no nimbus client ever created,
#    RemoteSettingsSyncWorker cannot sync the experiment collection no matter
#    how often WorkManager runs it. That is the acceptance criterion "no
#    remote-settings collection for experiments is synced", satisfied
#    structurally rather than by an allowlist. (The rest of the remote-settings
#    allowlist is LW-M4-08's; this patch deliberately does not touch the shared
#    RemoteSettingsService, which search config and the blocklist also use.)
#
# 2. `maybeFetchExperiments()` becomes a no-op.
#    Upstream it re-fetches on a `refreshIntervalForeground` schedule every time
#    the app comes to the foreground. That is the recurring beacon. The function
#    and its signature are kept so its one caller (FenixApplication.onForeground)
#    compiles unedited -- see "Files deliberately NOT touched" below.
#
# 3. `setFetchEnabled(false)` after build().
#    NimbusClient::fetch_experiments() checks DB_KEY_FETCH_ENABLED before it asks
#    the SettingsClient for anything, so this catches fetch call sites this patch
#    does not edit, e.g. MessageNotificationWorker's fetchExperiments(). Note this
#    does not hang that worker: Nimbus.fetchExperimentsOnThisThread() notifies
#    onExperimentsFetched() after fetch_experiments() returns, including on the
#    disabled early-return path, so tryFetchAndApplyNimbusExperiments() completes
#    normally instead of waiting out its timeout.
#
# And the LOCAL config channel, which is not a network fetch and is easy to miss:
# HomeActivity.onCreate calls `nimbus.sdk.initializeTooling(applicationContext,
# intent)` with the comment "This is a NOOP when launching normally". It is not a
# no-op on a release build. ArgumentProcessor.createCommandLineArgs(Uri) rejects
# only the http and https schemes; any other intent URI carrying
# `--nimbus-cli=true` is accepted, and `--experiments=<json>` is then fed to
# setExperimentsLocally() + applyPendingExperiments() and persisted to the Nimbus
# database, so it survives the launch and overrides the FML defaults on the next
# start -- fission.isolationStrategy included. With the remote channel severed
# that was the last remaining way for the FML defaults not to be the effective
# values, so the call and its import are removed. Nothing else in the tree calls
# initializeTooling() -- `grep -rn initializeTooling mobile/android/fenix/app/src`
# returns only this file after the patch -- so no source fails to compile.
# (Side effect, accepted: `app/src/androidTest/.../experimentintegration` is a
# pytest suite that drives the app with the external `nimbus-cli` binary, i.e.
# through exactly this hook, and will no longer work. It is a Mozilla QA suite
# for experiment enrolment, which this build has none of. It is Python, not
# compiled Kotlin, so it does not affect any build task.)
#
# Plus one that is about the FML defaults being authoritative rather than about
# the network: `initialExperiments = null`.
# res/raw/initial_experiments.json is a checked-in snapshot of live Mozilla
# experiment recipes -- 9 of them in 153.0, every one targeting the `release`
# channel, covering juno-onboarding, tab-groups, import-bookmarks and others.
# It is regenerated upstream by
# taskcluster/docker/periodic-updates/scripts/periodic_file_updates.sh.
# These are not inert: with no `.nimbus` file in the tree BuildConfig.NIMBUS_ENDPOINT
# is null, so AbstractNimbusBuilder.isLocalBuild() is true, so build() takes the
# applyLocalExperiments() branch and enrols from that file on EVERY launch, not
# just the first. Leaving it wired would mean nine Mozilla experiments still
# overriding the FML defaults in a browser that makes no network request -- the
# defaults would not be "the only source" at all. Null makes build() take
# applyPendingExperiments() instead, and with a NullClient nothing is ever
# pending. The JSON file itself is left in the tree, unread: emptying it would be
# a 720-line whole-file hunk that upstream's periodic job guarantees to break on
# every rebase, for no behavioural gain over not reading it.
#
#
# THE TEST CASUALTY (added by LW-M4-13, which owns this file)
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# mobile/android/fenix/app/src/test/java/org/mozilla/fenix/nimbus/NimbusSystemTest.kt
# is five unit tests whose whole subject is maybeFetchExperiments'
# refresh-interval behaviour -- exactly the behaviour the hunk below deletes.
# LW-M4-03 did not touch it and did not mention it.
#
# LW-M4-13's board entry says those tests "will not compile". That is WRONG, and
# it was checked rather than repeated: this patch keeps the function's name, its
# package (org.mozilla.fenix.experiments), its receiver (NimbusInterface) and its
# full signature including BOTH default arguments, so
# `import org.mozilla.fenix.experiments.maybeFetchExperiments` still resolves,
# and every other symbol the test file uses -- NimbusSystem,
# Settings.ONE_HOUR_MS / ONE_MINUTE_MS, Settings.nimbusUsePreview,
# Settings.nimbusLastFetchTime -- is untouched by this patch.
#
# What actually happens is a RED UNIT-TEST SUITE, not a red build, which is worse
# in the way that matters: it passes `assembleDebug` and only shows up if someone
# runs the tests. Four of the five assert `assertTrue(nimbus.isFetching)` after
# calling the now-no-op function, so they fail; the fifth ("before an interval
# THEN do not call fetchExperiments") passes vacuously, for the wrong reason.
# Three of the four would also throw on `lastTimeSlot.captured`, since the slot
# is never captured.
#
# The five are therefore REPLACED, not deleted, with the same five scenarios and
# the opposite expectation. A test that pins "this build never contacts an
# experiment server" is worth having: the single most likely way to lose this
# patch is an upstream rebase that restores the function body, and that is
# precisely what these tests now catch. Two assertions per test, not one --
# fetchExperiments() was not called AND Settings.nimbusLastFetchTime was not
# written -- because upstream records the timestamp *before* fetching, so a
# partial revert that kept the bookkeeping would otherwise slip through.
#
# One incidental cleanup in the same hunk: the test no longer mocks
# `context.components.settings` and passes the mocked Settings directly. The
# upstream version went through `org.mozilla.fenix.ext.components`, which is
# `applicationContext as FenixApplication` -- a cast on a relaxed mockk Context.
# Nothing in the assertions needed it.
#
# RUN, not reasoned about. `./mach gradle fenix:testDebugUnitTest --tests
# org.mozilla.fenix.nimbus.NimbusSystemTest` on a patched tree, inside the
# librewolf-android-build image and reusing the LW-M2-04 objdir and Gradle home:
# BUILD SUCCESSFUL, :fenix:compileDebugUnitTestKotlin EXECUTED (so the whole
# unit-test source set compiled, not just this file), and
# test-results/testDebugUnitTest/TEST-org.mozilla.fenix.nimbus.NimbusSystemTest.xml
# reads tests="5" skipped="0" failures="0" errors="0". Its captured stdout is
# eight lines of the logger.debug() line this patch puts in maybeFetchExperiments,
# which is independent evidence that the no-op body -- and not some other
# implementation -- is what the tests exercised. No other file under
# app/src/test or app/src/androidTest references maybeFetchExperiments,
# createNimbus, NimbusServerSettings, initial_experiments or initializeTooling,
# so this is the only test casualty of this patch.
#
#
# MEASURED ON THE DEVICE, WITH A NEGATIVE CONTROL (LW-M4-13)
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# The claim "no remote-settings collection for experiments is synced" is not
# checkable from a hostname capture: the nimbus collection is served from
# firefox.settings.services.mozilla.com, which this build contacts anyway for
# search config and quicksuggest. The Rust RemoteSettingsService gives a
# per-collection artefact instead -- Storage::new() opens one
# `<collection>.sql` under the app's app_remote-settings/ directory for every
# client make_client() creates (remote_settings/src/service.rs:78).
#
# Two x86_64 debug APKs, same emulator, same profile lifecycle, read with
# `adb shell run-as org.mozilla.fenix.debug`:
#
#   artefact                                  stock (LW-M2-04)   this patch
#   ----------------------------------------  -----------------  ----------
#   app_remote-settings/nimbus-mobile-        present, 243 KB    ABSENT
#     experiments.sql (+ -wal)                  of -wal
#   shared_prefs pref_key_nimbus_last_fetch   1787084596794      ABSENT
#   nimbus_data/db/data.safe.bin              237,603 bytes      1,951 bytes
#
# The other five collections (quicksuggest-amp, quicksuggest-other,
# search-config-icons, search-config-v2, search-telemetry-v2) are present in
# BOTH, which is what makes the nimbus row a measurement rather than a broken
# RemoteSettingsService.
#
# ONE ARTEFACT THAT LOOKS LIKE A FETCH AND IS NOT. `pref_key_nimbus_experiments_fetched`
# is `true` on the patched build too. It is NOT evidence of a network fetch:
# Nimbus.fetchExperimentsOnThisThread() (application-services
# .../nimbus/android/.../Nimbus.kt:305-315) calls nimbusClient.fetchExperiments()
# and then notifies onExperimentsFetched() UNCONDITIONALLY if that returned Ok --
# and the Rust fetch_experiments() returns Ok(()) immediately when
# is_fetch_enabled() is false (nimbus_client.rs:363-366), which barrier 3 above
# arranges. The flag is local bookkeeping read by SplashScreenOperation. Its
# only remaining caller is MessageNotificationWorker.setupNimbusMessaging().
# Do not "fix" this by chasing the flag; check nimbus-mobile-experiments.sql.
#
# THE INTERACTION THAT CUTS AGAINST THE OBVIOUS FIX (read this before rebasing)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# "Disable Nimbus, fall back to the FML defaults" would, done naively, LOCK IN
# the weakest possible process isolation. This is why the second hunk exists.
#
#   * Core.kt:207-208 reads
#     WebContentIsolationStrategy.fromValue(FxNimbus.features.fission.value().isolationStrategy)
#     UNCONDITIONALLY and hands it to GeckoRuntimeSettings, which commits it to
#     the `fission.webContentIsolationStrategy` pref at runtime
#     (GeckoRuntimeSettings.java:773-774, a PrefWithoutDefault -- landmine L2's
#     "runtime commit beats any default" shape).
#   * The FML default for that variable is 0 = ISOLATE_NOTHING on the release
#     channel, raised to 2 only for nightly and developer.
#   * Gecko's own default is 1 (modules/libpref/init/StaticPrefList.yaml,
#     `fission.webContentIsolationStrategy`).
#
# So on a stock release Fenix, Gecko's 1 is overwritten with 0 at startup, and
# once the remote channel is gone the FML default is the ONLY remaining input.
# Disabling Nimbus without touching it would cement isolate-nothing permanently.
#
# This patch therefore raises the release-channel FML default from 0 to 2
# (ISOLATE_HIGH_VALUE) in the same change, never as a follow-up. 2 is not a new
# policy invented here:
#   - upstream ships 2 on nightly and developer (the `defaults:` block, kept);
#   - android-components hardcodes ISOLATE_HIGH_VALUE as the default for every
#     non-Fenix GeckoView embedder (concept/engine Settings' DefaultSettings);
#   - GeckoWebContentIsolationMapper falls back to ISOLATE_HIGH_VALUE for any
#     unrecognised value;
#   - StaticPrefList's own comment says Android overrides this, "for Fenix via
#     nimbus.fml.yml, and for other Android Components consumers value 2 is
#     hardcoded".
# 1 (isolate every site-origin) is the desktop value and costs more RAM per
# site; choosing between 1 and 2 on a memory-constrained device is LW-M5-01's
# job and its acceptance requires a measurement on a 4GB device.
#
# CORRECTION (skeptical verification of LW-M4-03). This block used to end with
# "This patch only guarantees the value is never below what Gecko would have done
# on its own." That is FALSE, and it inverted the security claim.
# The strength order is 1 > 2 > 0:
#   1 = isolate everything  -- every site-origin gets its own process (strongest)
#   2 = isolate high value  -- only sites judged high value are isolated
#   0 = isolate nothing     -- effectively non-fission (weakest)
# Gecko's own default is 1 (StaticPrefList.yaml:6660-6662). This patch yields 2.
# So the effective isolation is WEAKER than Gecko's default, not "never below" it.
#
# What the patch does achieve, and it is the thing that mattered: it does NOT
# cement 0. The obvious implementation of "disable Nimbus, fall back to the
# compiled-in FML defaults" WOULD have, because the release-channel FML default
# for isolationStrategy is 0 -- so the naive fix would have shipped the weakest
# isolation from a patch whose purpose is privacy. Landing 2 instead of 0 is a
# real improvement over stock release Fenix; claiming it beats Gecko's default is
# not. LW-M5-01 owns the 1-vs-2 decision and must make it on measured RAM, with
# this corrected ordering in front of it.
#
# THE EFFECTIVE VALUE AFTER THIS PATCH IS 2 ON EVERY CHANNEL. Do not read that
# off the YAML: the FML is compiled by nimbus-fml into a Kotlin `FxNimbus`
# object per build variant, and the release variant is generated with
# `--channel release`, which is a different code path from the nightly one. See
# the verification note at the bottom for the generated-artefact check.
#
# `shouldUseNimbus` (default false) is NOT the escape hatch its description
# claims. Its description says "otherwise, the values from StaticPrefList.yaml
# apply"; that is false for isolationStrategy. It is read in exactly one place,
# GeckoProvider.kt:137, and gates only `.fissionEnabled()` -- never Core.kt's
# isolation-strategy line. This looks like an upstream bug and LW-M5-01's
# acceptance list already requires reporting it. Do not "fix" it here by
# flipping shouldUseNimbus to true: `enabled` defaults to false, so that would
# call `.fissionEnabled(false)` and disable fission outright, which is strictly
# worse than the problem.
#
#
# Files deliberately NOT touched, and why
# ---------------------------------------
#   * mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/components/Core.kt
#     -- owned by LW-M3-03 and LW-M5-01 as a tree_path. LW-M5-01's job is to make
#     Core.kt unable to LOWER the strategy; doing it here would collide.
#   * .../gecko/GeckoProvider.kt -- owned by LW-M5-02.
#   * .../FenixApplication.kt and .../components/NimbusComponents.kt -- reachable
#     alternatives (null the RemoteSettingsService at the call site, delete the
#     maybeFetchExperiments call) but each adds a file to the rebase surface for
#     no extra guarantee. The three network barriers all live in one file.
#   * third_party/application-services/components/nimbus/** -- the Rust SDK and
#     its Kotlin wrapper are left completely stock. Everything above is done by
#     choosing which arm of the SDK's own API to call, so an upstream refresh of
#     application-services cannot silently undo it; it can only make a hunk
#     reject.
#   * mobile/android/fenix/app/build.gradle -- BuildConfig.NIMBUS_ENDPOINT is
#     already null in this tree (it is read from a `.nimbus` file at the Gradle
#     rootDir, which does not exist), and in 153 that string is NOT what the SDK
#     fetches from anyway; it is read only by isLocalBuild(). Pinning it here
#     would look like the fix and would not be one.
#   * mobile/android/focus-android/** -- Focus has its own NimbusSetup.kt and is
#     not a LibreWolf product.
#   * Glean. RecordedNimbusContext and the NimbusEvents metrics still exist as
#     dead code here; removing the Glean upload path is LW-M4-01.
#
# Nothing happens outside this diff (landmine L4): no file copies, no rm, no
# generated file is edited by hand. The generated `FxNimbus` object is produced
# from the patched nimbus.fml.yaml at build time by the in-tree nimbus-fml
# (mobile/android/moz.configure `check_host_bin_prog("NIMBUS_FML", "nimbus-fml")`,
# built from services/app-services-tools/nimbus-fml), so the FML hunk reaches the
# APK through the normal build, not through a checked-in artefact.
#
# Ordering: no constraint. No other patch in common.txt, desktop.txt or
# android.txt touches any of the three files (checked with
#   grep -rn '^+++ b/mobile/' patches/
# whose only other hit is disable-data-reporting-android.patch's
# mobile/android/moz.configure). LW-M5-01's future
# fission-isolation.patch will share nimbus.fml.yaml and must be applied after
# this one; it already declares that ordering through depends_on: LW-M4-03.
#
# Kotlin note, measured rather than assumed.
# Two imports are removed: `org.mozilla.fenix.R` from NimbusSetup.kt (its only
# use was `R.raw.initial_experiments`) and
# `org.mozilla.experiments.nimbus.initializeTooling` from HomeActivity.kt. Every
# other import in the two files is still referenced -- NimbusServerSettings by
# the `NimbusServerSettings? = null` declaration, FxNimbusMessaging and
# NimbusSystem by maybeFetchExperiments' retained signature,
# RemoteSettingsService by createNimbus' retained parameter.
#
# The reason to be careful here looked like this: ProjectPlugin.kt's
# configureKotlinWarningsAsErrors() sets allWarningsAsErrors on EVERY
# KotlinCompile task in these projects, so anything kotlinc warns about is a
# build failure. That made the no-op maybeFetchExperiments(), which now has three
# unused parameters, and any leftover unused import, look like build breakage.
#
# They are not, and this was checked by running the compiler rather than by
# reasoning about it. kotlinc 2.3.20 (the Kotlin that ships in the Gradle 9.5.1
# distribution this build downloads; the tree pins 2.3.21) was run over a probe
# file containing an unused import, an unused local val, and unused parameters on
# a public top-level function, a public extension function with default arguments
# and a private function:
#   * plain and with -Werror  -> no diagnostics at all, exit 0
#   * with -Wextra            -> only "variable is unused", exit 0
#   * a @Deprecated call in the same probe DID warn, and -Werror DID turn that
#     one into "error: warnings found and -Werror specified", exit 1
# So the flag works and simply does not cover unused imports or unused
# parameters. The imports are still removed, because ktlint's no-unused-imports
# does flag them -- but that runs as the separate `ktlint` verification task
# (ProjectPlugin.configureKtlint), not as part of assemble.
#
# HOW THIS WAS VERIFIED, AND WHAT WAS NOT
# ---------------------------------------
# Be precise about this, because the acceptance criterion the board states --
# "no request to any experimenter/nimbus endpoint in a 10-minute capture" -- was
# NOT met by a capture. There is no APK yet (LW-M2-04 was still building one
# while this landed), so nothing could be run and nothing could be captured.
#
# Observed, by running something:
#
#  1. The whole android patch sequence (24 common + 5 android) applies to a
#     freshly extracted, real firefox-153.0esr tree:
#     `./scripts/check-patchfail.sh --targets=android` -> exit 0, no .rej.
#     Applied on its own with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0`, all three files of this patch
#     apply with zero fuzz.
#
#  2. The FML default really is 2 in the generated code, not just in the YAML.
#     The tree's own generator was built from the tree's own sources --
#     `cargo build --offline --locked --release -p toolchain-nimbus-fml`, the
#     crate mobile/android/moz.configure resolves NIMBUS_FML to -- and run with
#     exactly the arguments NimbusFeaturesTask.configureFmlCommand() passes:
#     `generate --language kotlin --channel <ch> --cache-dir <d> <manifest> <out>`.
#     In the generated org/mozilla/fenix/nimbus/FxNimbus.kt, class Fission:
#
#         channel     pristine 153.0esr        after this patch
#         release     isolationStrategy = 0    isolationStrategy = 2
#         beta        isolationStrategy = 0    isolationStrategy = 2
#         nightly     isolationStrategy = 2    isolationStrategy = 2
#         developer   isolationStrategy = 2    isolationStrategy = 2
#
#     Note `beta` was 0 as well; upstream's `defaults:` block only raises nightly
#     and developer, so two of the four channels shipped ISOLATE_NOTHING.
#     Diffing the two generated release-channel files, the ONLY non-comment line
#     that changes in the entire 2000-line file is that one default.
#     `nimbus-fml validate` on the patched manifest reports all four channels
#     valid (exit 0), so the `nimbusValidate` Gradle task that `nimbusFeatures*`
#     depends on will pass; `generate-experimenter` also succeeds.
#
#  3. The allWarningsAsErrors question above, by running kotlinc 2.3.20 on a
#     probe file. See the Kotlin note.
#
# NOT observed, and stated as static reasoning only:
#
#  * that no packet leaves the device. The three barriers are argued from the
#    application-services source, not from a capture. When an APK exists, the
#    capture to run is a 10-minute window covering first run, and the string to
#    grep for is `nimbus-mobile-experiments`, NOT `experimenter` -- see the note
#    at the top of this header. LW-M4-03's board `verify` line
#    (`... --network-capture | grep -c experimenter`) will return 0 on a STOCK
#    Fenix too and therefore proves nothing; that is a board bug, reported.
#  * that fission.webContentIsolationStrategy reads 2 in about:config on a
#    running build. That is LW-M5-01's acceptance and needs a device.
#  * that the Kotlin in this patch compiles. It has not been through a Kotlin
#    compiler with the real Fenix classpath, because no configured objdir for the
#    fenix subproject exists yet. The API surface each edited line uses was
#    checked against the declarations in this tree (NimbusInterface.setFetchEnabled,
#    AbstractNimbusBuilder.url/initialExperiments, Logger.debug), but "checked
#    against the declaration" is not "compiled".
